# What `docker build` must NOT send to the daemon, and therefore must not be
# able to land in a build-stage layer.
#
# 🔴 THIS IS A HYGIENE AND BUILD-TIME FILE, NOT A LEAK FIX FOR THE SHIPPED
# IMAGE, AND THE DISTINCTION IS WORTH STATING SO NOBODY RELAXES EITHER HALF.
# Stage 3 is `distroless/static` and copies exactly three binaries out of the
# build stage, so nothing matched below has ever reached a published layer.
# What `COPY . .` in stage 2 DOES cost is real anyway: the whole of `.git`
# (every object, every branch, every commit message) and whatever a developer
# happens to have sitting in their tree — a kubeconfig, a `.env`, a local
# `node_modules/` — are uploaded as build context and written into an
# intermediate layer that lives in the local builder cache and in any registry
# a `--target build` or a cache-export push ever sends it to.
#
# ⚠ SO THE RULE IS THE .gitignore RULE, INVERTED AND WITH ONE EXTRA CLAUSE:
# nothing `go build` or `make css-check` needs may appear here. .gitignore's own
# first line says nothing a build needs may be ignored; this file is read by a
# different tool with the same failure mode and a worse symptom — a missing
# build input here fails INSIDE the image build, where the error names a Go
# package rather than the line that excluded it.
#
# 🔴 web/static/app.css IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THIS FILE. It is committed,
# it is `go:embed`ed, and stage 1 rebuilds and `cmp`s it. Excluding it would
# break stage 1's comparison against the committed copy — which is the check
# that stops a stale stylesheet shipping.

# Version control. The largest single item in the context and the one with no
# use whatever inside the build: nothing in the Dockerfile reads git history,
# and VERSION is passed as a build arg precisely so it does not have to.
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Front-end tooling. Stage 1 runs its own `npm install` against a pinned
# package.json; a host node_modules/ would be uploaded for nothing and could
# shadow it with a different platform's binaries.
node_modules

# Local build outputs. The image builds its own from source; copying a host
# binary in would produce an image whose contents do not match its VERSION.
/muster
/muster-migrate
result
result-*

# Credentials and local environment. None of these are tracked, which is
# exactly why they are worth naming: a file git never sees is a file the
# build context picks up silently.
.env
.env.*
*.kubeconfig
kubeconfig
*-kubeconfig
*.pem
*.key
.secrets

# Python byte cache from the gates in tests/.
__pycache__
*.pyc

# Editor / OS droppings.
*.swp
.DS_Store
